+# CTR comparison and identification audit
+
+Comparison: 2026-09-21–2026-09-27 versus 2026-09-28–2026-10-04, inclusive. Read-only SQLite connection; no source data changed.
+
+No metrics.json, dashboard export, event-level data, experiment assignment, completion watermark, or rollback threshold was supplied. Define CTR provisionally as SUM(clicks)/SUM(searches), not the average of row CTRs. Whether clicks means distinct searches with a click is unverified. Observed date/segment coverage is complete through October 4; ingestion completeness cannot be independently verified.
+
+Let n[t,s] and c[t,s] denote searches and clicks for week t and platform-country stratum s; p=c/n, w=n/sum(n).
+
+- Observed change: sum(w[post]*p[post])-sum(w[pre]*p[pre]).
+- Adjusted change: sum(w[pre]*(p[post]-p[pre])). Baseline stratum weights are fixed.
+- Composition component: sum((w[post]-w[pre])*p[post]). Together with adjusted change this exactly reconstructs the observed change. This is an accounting decomposition, not a causal mediation analysis. Its value depends on the reference rates/weights.
+- Primary 95% intervals: estimate +/- 1.959964 standard errors. Observed variance is the sum of p_week*(1-p_week)/n_week. Adjusted variance is sum_s w[pre,s]^2 * sum_t p[t,s]*(1-p[t,s])/n[t,s]. Independent Bernoulli search outcomes and fixed weights are assumptions; repeat users, serial correlation, and multiple clicks cannot be assessed. For this database's fully enumerated rows the descriptive totals are exact; intervals quantify hypothetical sampling uncertainty under the stated model, not uncertainty about causal identification.
+- Sensitivity: repeat adjustment using post-period weights. Also pair each weekday with the same weekday in the following week; compute equal-day-weighted observed and baseline-stratum-weighted changes, then a t interval over seven independent pairs (6 degrees of freedom). These are slightly different estimands and cannot validate user-level independence or eliminate confounding.
+
+Interpretation: lower-CTR mobile searches gained share. The composition component is -2.089042 pp; the within-stratum component is -0.007791 pp. It is unsupported to call either component the effect of v2. The release may itself affect search frequency and hence composition. Calendar effects, query intent, acquisition mix within strata, and instrumentation changes are unmeasured. Four strata have point changes between -0.080380 and +0.051019 pp; these point estimates do not establish equivalence.
+
+The release log contains only the September 28 full rollout. All pre rows use v1, all post rows v2: version and period are perfectly confounded. No randomized holdout, overlap, untreated comparison group, or longer pre-trend exists here. A concurrent randomized v1/v2 comparison with stable logging and the intended search/user estimand would identify an effect under its design assumptions; a validated switchback is another option. An adjusted before/after difference would need untestable assumptions about absence of other within-stratum time changes and whether standardization conditions on treatment effects.
+
+Decision: the causal magnitude and interval are not identified, so neither rollback benefit nor v2 safety follows. No minimum material loss was supplied. For a purely descriptive benchmark, a loss threshold larger than 0.163342 pp lies outside the primary adjusted interval, while smaller thresholds may remain inside. This comparison cannot substitute for a causal decision interval.
+
+Checks: schema and all table contents inspected; 56 rows, 14 days, four strata per day, no duplicate keys or invalid/null counts/dimensions; both periods contain the same weekdays; no concurrent version overlap. Source reconciliation and ingestion-watermark validation could not run. The initial `python` executable was unavailable; rerun successfully with `python3`.
+
+Reproduce with `python3 analysis_output/analyze.py`. Exact SQL and result rows are in queries_and_results.json; numeric outputs, checks, interval sensitivity, and the database SHA-256 are in results.json.
